@startuml openengine-simple
title How OpenEngine works today — simplified example
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontSize 14
skinparam activityBackgroundColor #EDF3FA
skinparam activityBorderColor #557799

start
:Start a run of a graph;

partition "Graph: steps connected in an order" {
  :Step 1: Prepare a workspace;

  partition "Step 2: Agent node — for example, implement a change" {
    :Build a prompt from selected graph state;
    :langgraph-acp connects to the selected agent\nCodex OR Claude, through its ACP adapter;
    :Create an agent session\n(or load an existing session when recovering);
    :Send the prompt through ACP;
    :Agent works in the workspace\nand streams responses / tool updates;
    :OpenEngine handles permission requests\nand records events;
    :Write the node's result into graph state;
  }

  :Step 3: Run checks;
  :Step 4: Review the result\n(can be another Codex or Claude agent node);
}

:Finish the run;
stop

note right
  This is an example graph, not a fixed pipeline.
  Each graph defines its own steps and connections.
  Graphs can also branch, repeat steps, or run steps in parallel.

  Only agent nodes need ACP.
  Other nodes can run ordinary application code.
end note

legend bottom
  Graph = workflow definition: steps and connections.
  Run = one execution of that graph.
  Graph state = data passed between steps.
  Agent session = conversation held by Codex or Claude.
  ACP = protocol used to communicate with that agent.

  Today, the existing ACP providers launch agents on the host.
  Moving agent execution inside a VM is the proposed change.
endlegend
@enduml
